{"parentUuid":"0df3ff27-9daa-42ca-adfd-4b21fb32e574","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_016RBsdPzs9cUqa1LnjL55Yu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent — concrete data. Three key takeaways from these logs:\n\n**1. SessionStart doubles (3ms apart)** — Claude Code first (16:47:10.405), then Cursor (16:47:10.408). Hint dedup handles this.\n\n**2. TurnStart fires once — only Cursor** (16:47:11.040). No Claude Code TurnStart anywhere in the log.\n\nThis is a big finding. It means the `InitializeSession` race the PR was originally designed around **doesn't actually happen in this scenario**. Cursor IDE doesn't forward `UserPromptSubmit`/`beforeSubmitPrompt` to `.claude/settings.json` — only `SessionStart` and `Stop` (TurnEnd). So the per-session lock the PR added was protecting a race that wasn't real in practice. Dropping it (which we just did) is the right call — and the empirical justification is right here in the logs.\n\n**3. TurnEnd doubles (21ms apart)** — Claude Code at 16:47:14.482, Cursor at 16:47:14.503. **Both have the same `session_ref`** (Cursor's transcript path, in the nested-folder layout). The PR doesn't address this at all.\n\nIn this specific turn it was a no-op because no files were modified, so both skipped checkpoint creation. In a turn with file changes, both processes would:\n- Run `SaveStep` (file detection, attribution, tree building)\n- Write a shadow branch commit\n- Write to `.entire/metadata/<sid>/full.jsonl`\n- Both with identical input but as concurrent writers\n\nThat's the race that's actually still active. Concrete consequences I'd expect:\n- Two shadow-branch commits per turn (doubled `step_count`)\n- Two `full.jsonl` writes (last writer wins, possibly torn write under heavy turns)\n- Doubled per-turn cost/duration aggregation if the metadata logs both\n- Subtle: the Claude Code process at 16:47:17.499 hits a `transcript flush sentinel not found within timeout` warning — it's trying to read its own transcript flush marker but the path is *Cursor's* transcript, so the sentinel will never appear. It just times out and proceeds.\n\n**Recommended next step (not in this PR):**\n\nGeneralize the dedup at the lifecycle dispatcher level. After SessionStart claims the hint for, say, \"Cursor,\" any subsequent hook event that fires for that session_id from a different `ag.Type()` should short-circuit early — log it, no-op, return. That's one check at the top of `DispatchLifecycleEvent` and it covers TurnEnd, SubagentEnd, Compaction, SessionEnd uniformly.\n\nPseudocode for the generalization:\n\n```go\nif hint := strategy.LoadAgentTypeHint(ctx, event.SessionID); hint != \"\" && hint != ag.Type() {\n    logging.Info(logCtx, \"skipping forwarded hook for non-owning agent\",\n        slog.String(\"hint_owner\", string(hint)),\n        slog.String(\"firing_agent\", string(ag.Type())),\n        slog.String(\"event\", event.Type.String()),\n    )\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nPlace it right after the nil-checks at the top of `DispatchLifecycleEvent`. SessionStart itself stays exempt (the hint isn't written until inside `handleLifecycleSessionStart`). The transcript-path safety net in `InitializeSession` becomes redundant but harmless to keep as belt-and-suspenders.\n\nThat's the durable fix — it doesn't depend on enumerating every event type or reasoning about which ones the IDE happens to forward today. If Cursor adds forwarding for a new event tomorrow, this still holds.\n\nWorth a follow-up PR. The current PR (with our changes) is still a net improvement; this just acknowledges what the logs actually show."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":190586,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7398,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":190586,"ephemeral_5m