es all of those redundancies, not just the new one.\n\n---\n\n**File: cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go:54**\n\n**Issue:** Minor: condition order is fine — `event.Type` comparisons are cheap and short-circuit before the `event.SessionID != \"\"` string compare. Not a real concern.\n\n---\n\n**File: cmd/entire/cli/lifecycle.go:55**\n\n**Issue:** The error from `LoadSessionState` is swallowed via `_`. If the state file is briefly unreadable (concurrent rename from `SaveSessionState`'s tmp→final), the dispatcher falls through to normal dispatch — fine for correctness, but the silent error means observability is lost. The `nolint:errcheck` comment acknowledges this; fine as-is, but consider a debug-level log on non-`os.IsNotExist` errors so transient FS issues are diagnosable.\n\n---\n\n**File: cmd/entire/cli/strategy/manual_commit_hooks.go:617 (`InitializeSession`)**\n\n**Issue:** `InitializeSession` now calls `resolveSessionAgentType` (which calls `LoadAgentTypeHint` — a small disk read) and then immediately calls `s.loadSessionState`, then in the existing-session branch may call `correctSessionAgentType` (which calls `paths.WorktreeRoot` again — already resolved by `OpenRepository` above). Not a hot-path explosion, but `paths.WorktreeRoot` is called twice per TurnStart now. Caching the worktree root in the function (already cached globally per `paths.ClearWorktreeRootCache` in tests) likely makes this a non-issue in practice — confirm `WorktreeRoot` is memoized, otherwise pass the resolved root through.\n\n---\n\n**Concurrency/memory:** No leaked goroutines, no unbounded state, no missing cleanup introduced. The lock removal is clean — `InitializeSession`'s comment explicitly accepts the race and documents convergence via `correctSessionAgentType`.\n\n**Bottom line:** The dispatcher's new `LoadSessionState` call is the one real hot-path concern. Every lifecycle event now pays for one extra disk read + git-common-dir resolution, and downstream handlers immediately re-load the same file 1–4 more times. A request-scoped cache (context value) would eliminate both the new redundancy and several pre-existing ones.</result>\n<usage><total_tokens>72089</total_tokens><tool_uses>5</tool_uses><duration_ms>40880</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
